0|0@xboxone^1440777112#cuiq5oy:Gadafro$:I like it, been playing with a friend who normally dislikes CoD games, but he really enjoys it as well. I like the movement - compared to AW's movement, its less jumpy, it has a focus on smooth movements with gradual transitions rather than an instant twitch movement and blink-like strafes. To that end, I feel its easier to control but not as effective at evading fights compared to AW's, both are bonuses in my books. Wall-running for example makes you especially vulnerable to those who aren't wall-running and sliding doesn't instantly win a fight. As for the maps, I feel they fit well with the movement. They're small and arena based, which I like. I do hope they have some large scale maps though, I miss them from the likes of World at War and the first Modern Warfare. That said, I do have one gripe - after playing Titanfall yet still having its pseudo-movement system in BO3, I keep finding ways to get to the heights of buildings, but then I am prevented from standing on said height due to an invisible wall. It irks me a little that invisible walls are so prominent and noticeable; these rooftops aren't game-breaking, so why not allows us to go there and extend the map vertically? That just seems like a poor design choice in my opinion, but that's really my only complaint so far. There will clearly be some balancing done (ex: Sparrow) but all in all, I think I wont be selling BO3 on after a couple of months with it, it might be a long term CoD game for me. 
2|0@xboxone^1440782852#cuityzb:StrikeAnywherePanda$:&gt; I keep finding ways to get to the heights of buildings, but then I am prevented from standing on said height due to an invisible wall. My brother pointed that out to me, and I felt like I couldn't give a decent explanation as to why. I guess I could also say that is the only thing that seems to bother me. Also they put a large emphasis on the wall running, which is cool, but the walls aren't all over the place as I was hoping. That could just be me bitching though.
1|0@xboxone^1440791472#cuizrz0:xboxonelosty$:I think they are trying to keep the game more ground based. It's hard to explain, but Treyarch's games tend to have less height advantages than some of the other games in the series. With the new jumping ability, they used invisible walls to keep people closer to the ground. Edit: BTW I'm not saying there aren't buildings and height advantages in Black Ops, but it appears to be less than other COD games. You could jump almost anywhere in Advanced Warfare. The original Modern Warfares had multiple big buildings you could climb and camp in. 
2|0@xboxone^1440791678#cuizx12:StrikeAnywherePanda$:I know that's what they are trying to do, but then maybe they should have thought about level layout and settings a little bit more. Or at least design the buildings a certain way where there would be a legitimate reason that you can't get on top of it. Putting barbed wire on it isn't a good enough reason to say "You can't go here."
